            """Industrial location in India today reflects raw materials (steel near ore/coal), ports and logistics (coastal petrochemicals, electronics assembly), skilled labour and agglomeration (Bengaluru IT, Tiruppur textiles), power availability, land and environmental clearances, state incentives (industrial corridors, PLI), and proximity to markets. Special Economic Zones and highway connectivity increasingly outweigh pure raw-material pull. Political stability and ease of doing business also shape footloose investment.""",
            """Agriculture expands by clearing forests; urbanization fragments habitats and creates pollution sinks. Western Ghats mining/plantations, Northeast jhum pressures, and Central Indian corridor fragmentation harm wildlife (elephant/tiger landscapes). Wetland loss around cities reduces biodiversity and flood buffers. The statement is largely valid, though extractive industry and infrastructure also drive degradation. Mitigation needs land-use planning, community forest rights done right, green belts, and ecological compensation that is real, not paper.""",
