            """A living constitution adapts through amendment, judicial interpretation, and convention while retaining core identity. India’s document has evolved via the basic structure doctrine, rights expansion (privacy, education), and institutional innovation, responding to social change without needing revolution. “Living” does not mean unbounded: amendment power is wide yet limited. The metaphor captures flexibility with continuity — essential for a diverse democracy.""",
            """Article 360 allows financial emergency if financial stability or credit of India is threatened: Centre may reduce salaries, require money bills reserved, and direct states on financial propriety. Never imposed, it remains a potent centralizing tool. Effects would sharply curtail state fiscal autonomy. Critical view: necessary nuclear option for extreme crisis, but prone to political misuse if thresholds are vague; parliamentary and judicial vigilance would be essential.""",
            """Rajya Sabha’s special powers include exclusive initiation of Article 249/312-type measures in some federal domains historically associated with Council of States role, approving all-India services creation, and equal footing on constitutional amendments and many non-money bills. It cannot be bypassed indefinitely on ordinary legislation without Joint Sitting limits nuances, and money bills curtail it. These features give federal and deliberative prestige beyond mere second-chamber delay.""",
            """Supreme Court writs (Art. 32) protect fundamental rights and are themselves a fundamental right; High Courts (Art. 226) have wider sweep — FR plus other legal rights — but SC is the apex with pan-India authority. HC territorial limits and SC’s broader appellate/constitutional role differ. Both issue habeas corpus, mandamus, prohibition, certiorari, quo warranto; accessibility and scope make 226 a powerful everyday remedy.""",
            """COVID response used the Disaster Management Act and epidemic laws with strong Union steering of lockdowns, oxygen/vaccine allocation, and fiscal packages, while states implemented on the ground. Centrally defined rules and GST compensation stresses illustrated vertical imbalance. Cooperative federalism rhetoric coexisted with command-and-control moments. Examples: nationwide lockdown announcement, vaccine procurement centralization early on, and differential state models later — a temporary but real tilt toward the Centre.""",
